    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Islamic tradition traces the city to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a>, Hagar and Ishmael and the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>, while historians point out that sources for its early history are sparse and that the first clear non-Islamic reference dates from 741.</li><li>Muhammad was born there in 570 and, according to Islamic tradition, received his first revelation in a cave on a nearby mountain in 610. The city came under Muslim control in 630.</li><li>Mecca was rarely a political capital, but pilgrims kept it important, and it passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> and sharifs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, the Hashemite Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> and, in 1924 and 1925, the Saudi state.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A study by Majied Robinson, based on early Islamic genealogical records, estimates that Mecca had about 550 people at the time of Muhammad, while Muslim scholars using traditional sources place the number as high as 10,000.</p></aside>
    </section>

