    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">5th century</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> take control of Mecca</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">570</span><span class="rf-text">Muhammad is born in Mecca; Islamic sources place the failed expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraha">Abraha</a> in the same year</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">622</span><span class="rf-text">Muhammad emigrates with his companions to Yathrib, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">630</span><span class="rf-text">Muhammad and about 10,000 followers enter and take the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">930</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a> attack and sack Mecca</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1517</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-of-mecca">Sharif of Mecca</a> acknowledges the supremacy of the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1916</span><span class="rf-text">The revolt of Sharif Hussain bin Ali takes Mecca, and he proclaims the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1924</span><span class="rf-text">The Saud family takes Mecca and overthrows the sharif</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kaaba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaaba.jpg" alt="Kaaba" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ka%27ba,_Great_Mosque_of_Mecca,_Saudi_Arabia_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Mortel, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the Islamic view the beginnings of Mecca go back to Adam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a>, Hagar and Ishmael. According to this account Abraham left Hagar and his son Ishmael in the valley at God's command, members of the Yemeni tribe of Jurhum settled with them, and Abraham and Ishmael built or, in other Islamic accounts, rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>. Islamic scholars differ on who first built it: some hold that angels did, others that Adam did. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> names the city Bakkah in Surah 3, verse 96, and Umm al-Qura in 6:92, and an Islamic suggestion identifies the desert of Paran in Genesis with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tihama">Tihama</a> plain where Ishmael is held to have settled.</p><p>Historians describe the early history as largely shrouded by a lack of clear sources. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> was remote, rocky and thinly settled, and its people were illiterate, so there are few inscriptions or accounts by Greek or Roman writers. The first clear reference in non-Islamic literature is the Chronicle of 741, though Tom Holland claims that its author places the region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Possible earlier mentions are disputed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> described a holy shrine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> in the first century BCE, but the place he describes lies in the north-west, in the Nabataean kingdom. Ptolemy's Macoraba was long taken to be Mecca, and Glen Bowersock still favours that, while Patricia Crone wrote that the name has nothing to do with Mecca. The etymology of the name is obscure.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mecca&#8221; cites where it covers Etymology, Other names, Early history (up to 6th century CE):</p><ul><li>Crone, Patricia, Meccan Trade and the Rise of Islam (Princeton University Press, 1987)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent.jpg" alt="Suleiman the Magnificent" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EmperorSuleiman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Circle of Titian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> took control of Mecca in the 5th century and became skilled merchants. In the 6th century they joined the spice trade as sea routes grew dangerous and overland routes grew safer. The area was barren, but Mecca was the wealthiest of three settlements on the south-western coast, with water from the Zamzam <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Well</a> and a position on major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a>. Goods such as leather, livestock and metals were carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the Far East are said to have passed through. Mecca became the centre of a loose confederation of client tribes. Once a year the tribes declared a truce and gathered there on pilgrimage, to the shrine, to drink the Zamzam, to settle disputes and debts, and to trade at fairs.</p><p>Historians disagree about the scale of the trade. Patricia Crone and Tom Holland cast doubt on the claim that Mecca was a major trading outpost, while Glen Bowersock asserts that it was. Crone argued that its trade rested on skins, hides, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leather">leather goods</a>, clarified butter, woollens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, largely for the Roman army.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread, Mecca attracted scholars, pious Muslims who wished to live near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>, and local people who served the pilgrims. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zubaidah-bint-jafar">Zubayda</a>, wife of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Harun ar-Rashid, paid for the deepening of the Zamzam Well and an aqueduct, in a project likely to have cost 1.75 million gold dinars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> rebuilt the aqueduct between 1525 and 1571.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mecca&#8221; cites where it covers Medieval and pre-modern times, Demographics:</p><ul><li>Ziauddin Sardar, Mecca: The Sacred City (Bloomsbury, 2014)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Muhammad Ali of Egypt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ModernEgypt,_Muhammad_Ali_by_Auguste_Couder,_BAP_17996.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Auguste Couder, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> ruled the city, and their most important deity, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubal">Hubal</a>, was placed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>. Muhammad was born into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a>, a faction of the Quraysh. According to Islamic tradition, he received revelations in 610 through the archangel Jibreel, and after 13 years of persecution he emigrated to Yathrib, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in 622. In 628 the Quraysh blocked the Muslims' pilgrimage, and the Treaty of al-Hudaybiya promised a ten-year ceasefire. After the Banu Bakr, allies of the Quraysh, attacked a group of the Banu Khuza'ah, allies of the Muslims, Muhammad marched into Mecca with 10,000 men and took it in 630. The pagan imagery was destroyed, and Attab ibn Asid was made governor.</p><p>Mecca was never the capital of an Islamic state except the Zubayrid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a>. Ali chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. The Umayyads besieged the city in 683 and 692 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-fitna">Second Fitna</a>. In 1517 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-of-mecca">Sharif of Mecca</a>, Barakat bin Muhammad, acknowledged the Ottoman Caliph but kept much local autonomy. The First Saudi state held the city from 1803 to 1813, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali Pasha</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> returned it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1813. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> Hussain bin Ali, the Sharif, revolted with the British officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-e-lawrence">T. E. Lawrence</a>, took Mecca in 1916 and declared the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> with Mecca as capital. After the capture of Mecca in 1924 the Saud family overthrew the sharif, and another passage dates the Saudi conquest of the Hejaz to 1925.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mecca&#8221; cites where it covers Muhammad and the conquest of Mecca:</p><ul><li>F. E. Peters, The Hajj: The Muslim Pilgrimage to Mecca and the Holy Places (Princeton University Press, 1994)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/black-stone"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-stone.jpg" alt="Black Stone" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/black-stone">Black Stone</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_stone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masjid-al-haram">Masjid al-Haram</a> holds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>, the direction of prayer for all Muslims, and the Zamzam <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Well</a>. In Islamic tradition the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-stone">Black Stone</a> was sent down from Paradise and was once whiter than milk; scientists consider it a meteorite or something of similar origin. Pilgrims circle the Kaaba, in the rite called tawaf, and walk seven times between the hills of Safa and Marwa. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a>, which every adult Muslim with the means must perform at least once, is held in the twelfth month, Dhu al-Hijjah. In 2019 it drew 2,489,406 pilgrims. The 'Umrah, the lesser pilgrimage, may be made at any time. Pilgrims also visit Mina, Muzdalifah and Mount Arafat.</p><p>Pilgrims came by boat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a>, overland or with the annual caravans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and many stayed. By the 19th century people of South Asian origin made up 20 per cent of the population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> stayed from about 1327 to 1330 and described the people as humble, kind and clean. Non-Muslims may not enter the city. The first recorded non-Muslim to do so was Ludovico di Varthema of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> in 1503, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a> is said to have visited in December 1518, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-francis-burton">Richard Francis Burton</a> made the pilgrimage in 1853 disguised as a Muslim. The first press arrived in 1885, and the first major school, the Madrasat al-Falah, was founded in 1911 or 1912.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abraha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraha.jpg" alt="Abraha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abraha">Abraha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balami_-_Tarikhnama_-_The_miraculous_rescue_of_the_Ka%27ba_from_Abraha%27s_attack_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Abu Ali Bal'ami, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Islamic sources place the attack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraha">Abraha</a>, a Yemeni ruler, in the Year of the Elephant, about 570 to 572. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>'s Surah al-Fil, birds sent by God drove off his army, and Islamic tradition says this was the year of Muhammad's birth. It has been theorised that an epidemic such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> caused the failure. Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr held Mecca during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-fitna">Second Fitna</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> besieged it in 683 and 692. In 930 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a>, a Shi'a Isma'ili sect led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-tahir-al-jannabi">Abu Tahir al-Jannabi</a>, attacked and sacked the city. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> reached it in 1349, and cholera broke out among pilgrims 27 times between 1830 and 1930.</p><p>Water was scarce, and floods came as well: al-Kurdi counted 89 by 1965, the worst in the last century in 1942. In 1803 the First Saudi state destroyed some historic tombs and domes in and around the city.</p><p>On 20 November 1979 about two hundred armed men led by Juhayman al-Otaybi seized the Grand Mosque, claiming that the Saudi royal family no longer represented pure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. They took tens of thousands of pilgrims hostage. The siege lasted two weeks and ended when a multinational force retook the mosque, with several hundred deaths and damage to the shrine. On 2 July 1990 the ventilation failed in a crowded pedestrian tunnel and 1,426 people suffocated or were trampled. On 24 September 2015, 700 pilgrims were killed in a stampede at Mina.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/masjid-al-haram"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masjid-al-haram.jpg" alt="Masjid al-Haram" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/masjid-al-haram">Masjid al-Haram</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edited_Great_Mosque_of_Mecca1_5-2019-ccsa4.0_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Saudipics.com, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mecca is the holiest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in all its branches. Its importance comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a>, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/five-pillars-of-islam">Five Pillars of Islam</a>, from the 'Umrah, and from its status as the birthplace of Muhammad. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-nour">Cave of Hira</a> on Jabal al-Nour is where Muslims believe the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> was first revealed to him, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a> is where they pray towards. In Islamic tradition a prayer in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masjid-al-haram">Masjid al-Haram</a> is worth 100,000 prayers in any other mosque.</p><p>The built city has changed greatly under Saudi rule. It has been estimated that since 1985 about 95 per cent of Mecca's historic buildings, most over a thousand years old, have been demolished, and that fewer than 20 structures from the time of Muhammad remain. They include the houses of Khadijah and Abu Bakr, the reported birthplace of Muhammad and the Ottoman Ajyad Fortress. Two reasons are given: the Saudi government feared that such sites might become places of association in worship beside God, and many demolitions were officially for expanding the Masjid al-Haram and building hotels, apartments, car parks and other facilities for pilgrims. The city now covers over 1,200 square kilometres and its metropolitan area holds about 2.4 million people.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/tala-al-badru-alayna">Tala al-Badru Alayna<span>c. 622</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mecca is the holiest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, tied by tradition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> and Ishmael and the birthplace of Muhammad. Ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, it came under Muslim control in 630 and later under sharifs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the Saudi state, and the pilgrimage still defines it.</em></p>

